{"text": "Apple’s working-capital cycle is still deeply negative, with the latest quarter at -227.86 [f_e8d497fd507e@2026-03-28]. Over the last twelve quarters, days sales outstanding moved from 87.23 [f_845064344cc6@2023-07-01] to 99.60 [f_845064344cc6@2026-03-28], days inventory moved from 59.12 [f_8e4be2f2f72d@2023-07-01] to 43.66 [f_8e4be2f2f72d@2026-03-28], days payable moved from 375.58 [f_d9c9ede2d256@2023-07-01] to 371.12 [f_d9c9ede2d256@2026-03-28], and cash conversion cycle moved from -229.22 [f_e8d497fd507e@2023-07-01] to -227.86 [f_e8d497fd507e@2026-03-28]. The full quarterly series is [chart: cash_conversion_cycle_aapl].

Against its three-year range, the cycle’s low was -75.83 [f_8eb7e20434fb@2024-09-28] and its high was -67.83 [f_8eb7e20434fb@2023-09-30], so the latest -71.07 [f_8eb7e20434fb@2025-09-27] sat inside that band, closer to the low than the high. The three-year series is [chart: cash_conversion_cycle_aapl].

On supplier-financing wording, the latest 10-K passage I received does not mention supplier financing, vendor financing, supply chain financing, or reverse factoring at all. The passage instead says tariffs and other measures can adversely affect “the Company’s supply chain” and “pricing and gross margin” [f_93c598bc243b]. I could not get the prior 10-K wording from the evidence returned, so I cannot say the wording changed; what I can say is that the latest 10-K Item 7 excerpt provided here contains no supplier-financing language."}

{"text": "The worst drawdown episode over the last year was a decline of 12.0% [f_4177f38708ba]. The desk’s explanation pins that episode to the window from 2026-01-07 to 2026-03-27, with the portfolio return in that span at -12.0% [f_78e6f38708ba]. I could not get the desk to return the episode’s peak date or a holdings contribution breakdown, because it said, “book.explain_episode needs a peak and a trough: ask for the worst drawdown episode and its explanation in words (`ask`)” and then “$explain holds no column holdings.<label>.contribution”.

On the filing question, yes: at least some of the holdings had filings in that window. The desk returned Item 7 filings for AAPL [f_c480a72fb4a9], JPM [f_98f53b60a82d], LLY [f_9e2d8a64d8c4], MSFT [f_bb1d42d23495], GOOGL [f_b94924932824], AMZN [f_a73ca0b11372], XOM [f_cc826e7d58d6], and NVDA [f_6943f8bb77b1]. It could not return Item 7 for HYG and TLT, saying “section_not_found.”

What this means for the book is that the drawdown was real and material, but I do not yet have the desk’s contributor attribution for the episode, so I cannot say which three names drove it most. The filings tell us the episode overlapped with a period in which several held names had fresh annual discussion/MD&A disclosures, but without the contribution breakdown I cannot connect those filings to the drawdown or say they changed the reading."}

