{"text": "AWS was 17% [f_210cf2ef002b] of Amazon’s net sales in 2024 and 18% [f_210cf2ef002b] in 2025, based on AWS net sales of $107,556 [f_210cf2ef002b] and $128,725 [f_210cf2ef002b] against consolidated net sales of $637,959 [f_210cf2ef002b] and $716,924 [f_210cf2ef002b]. The underlying filing passage says: “Net Sales Mix: North America61 %59 % International22 23 AWS17 18 Consolidated100 %100 %” [f_210cf2ef002b].

Over the three annual points the desk gave, AWS net sales moved from $575B [f_71cadeaacf78@2023-12-31] to $638B [f_71cadeaacf78@2024-12-31] to $717B [f_71cadeaacf78@2025-12-31], and the same three points for consolidated revenue are the same figures [f_eec3df3d5030@2023-12-31] [f_eec3df3d5030@2024-12-31] [f_eec3df3d5030@2025-12-31]. On that basis, AWS’s share moved from 16.0% [f_71cadeaacf78@2023-12-31] to 16.9% [f_71cadeaacf78@2024-12-31] to 17.9% [f_71cadeaacf78@2025-12-31].

For the book, AMZN is 7.03% [f_2a031df9da13] of port_001. The desk could give AMZN’s last-1y return versus SPY as -8.12% [f_a60dada818b6], but it could not provide a run-based reconciliation for AMZN because “book.reconcile is a run method; name a run in subjects” [f_9d25d72cee80]. So I can say AMZN itself underperformed SPY over the last year by -8.12% [f_a60dada818b6], but I cannot attribute that underperformance specifically to AWS from the desk evidence. That means the AWS share trend is useful for business-mix context, not for explaining the stock’s relative return."}

